<china@thewrittenword.com> wrote in message 
news:idadnTiEpbuXFPbenZ2dnUVZ_tOdnZ2d@speakeasy.net...
> When compiling C++ programs with -model ansi, libcxxma gets linked in
> rather than libcxx ('ma' in libcxxma = 'Model Ansi' I presume). Why
> isn't libcxxma.so included in CXXREDIST651V37? A bug?
>

The model ansi object model was added in Version 6.3 of the C++ compiler
which became available in early 2001.  Our intentions were to roll this
out and to have isv's make their libraries available in both ansi and arm
object models and to eventually make our libraries available in the
redistribution kit.  In the manpage, we also stated (as a limitation
or warning) that users choosing to use model ansi would have to have
access to all of their sources and recompile them with a consistent
object model.

The complete rollout of model ansi with isv's was not completed due to
the Alpha announcement that was made in June 2001.  Model ansi continues
to be available and supported, but the libraries are not included in
the redistribution kit.  If you are distributing libraries you should
not assume that your customer can use the model ansi object model due
to a dependency on other vendor libraries.  If you are distributing
complete binaries, I would suggest that you link against the archive
library which would eliminate the need to have the libcxxma.so in
the redistribution kit.

Ed Vogel

HP/Compaq/DEC C/C++ Engineering.




